Name:           malachi
Version:        @VERSION@
Release:        1%{?dist}
Summary:        Mail client for the GNOME desktop

# The daemon and its API are AGPL-3.0-only (dual-licensed, see LICENSING.md);
# the GTK interface and everything else GPL-3.0-or-later. malachi-mcp also
# carries third-party code under permissive licences (its Go modules and
# the PDFium it embeds), listed in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md, which
# scripts/build-rpm.sh marks as a licence file beside LICENSE and
# LICENSING.md; this tag names the project's own licences.
License:        AGPL-3.0-only AND GPL-3.0-or-later
URL:            https://github.com/schotek/malachi

# The binaries are built before rpmbuild is called (scripts/build-rpm.sh) and
# only staged here, so there is nothing to compile and no source archive.
BuildArch:      @ARCH@
AutoReqProv:    yes

# What no ELF header can say. The rest of the dependencies come from the
# automatic ELF scan, which is the RPM counterpart of dpkg-shlibdeps.
Requires:       libadwaita >= 1.7
Requires:       dconf

Recommends:     xdg-desktop-portal
Recommends:     gnome-keyring
Suggests:       evolution-data-server
Suggests:       gnome-online-accounts

# Go binaries carry their own debug information and the extraction produces
# nothing useful here; without this rpmbuild fails on the stripped tree.
%global debug_package %{nil}

%description
Malachi Mail is two processes: a Go daemon that speaks IMAP, SMTP,
Microsoft Graph, the Jira REST API and CalDAV and owns every mail
decision, and a GTK4 user interface that talks to it over a unix socket.
The interface starts the daemon itself; nothing needs to be enabled by
hand. The same daemon drives the native Malachi Mail apps for macOS and
Windows.

Mail is kept offline, grouped into conversations and searched; Jira
issues are followed as conversations; a calendar keeps Microsoft 365 and
CalDAV calendars and answers invitations from the message; the Board
sorts what waits for you, what waits for others and what is just for
your information.

Received HTML is sanitised in the daemon and shown in a WebKitGTK view
with JavaScript off, no network access and links opened only through the
portal after the real destination has been displayed.

Passwords and OAuth2 tokens are kept in the system keyring. Microsoft 365
signs in in the browser with the public client registration the daemon
ships, Gmail with an app password or a Google client of your own; on
GNOME both can also come from GNOME Online Accounts.

The package also installs malachi-mcp, a Model Context Protocol server
that gives an AI agent a gated view of the mail, the calendar and the
board through the daemon. It is read-only unless its flags say otherwise
and is never started on its own.

%install
cp -a @STAGE@/. %{buildroot}/

# The list is generated from the staged tree by scripts/build-rpm.sh rather
# than written out here. Repeating it would make this a second copy of the
# install manifest in scripts/build.sh, free to drift from it: the symbolic
# icon and the set of translations are both conditional there, and a spec
# that names them fails the build the day one of them is not produced.
%files -f @FILELIST@

%changelog
# One generated entry, not a history: the history lives in git and in the
# GitHub release notes, and copying it here would only let the two drift.
# rpm insists on the tag existing at all, hence this.
* @DATE@ Vladislav Janeček <38503981+schotek@users.noreply.github.com> - @VERSION@-1
- Built from git @VERSION@; see the GitHub release notes for what changed.
